is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The failure is not in Location A’s router0 routing process; it’s an L3 policy (firewall) on router6 at Location C blocking transit traffic. Symptom mechanism: from PC4/PC5, you can reach router6 itself (ICMP to 10.50.1.2 and 172.18.51.254 succeeds), but any host behind it (e.g., 172.18.51.17) is unreachable. Traceroute from PC4 toward 172.18.51.17 reaches 10.50.1.2 (router6) then stops, proving forwarding at router6 is the choke point. Router0 has healthy RIP routes (show ip route contains learned and connected prefixes), and it can reach the next hops (ping to 10.16.1.2 OK). Router6 can ping both PC4 and local 172.18.51.x hosts, but hosts cannot communicate across router6 because its FORWARD chain drops anything entering eth2: iptables shows “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP; -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP; -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP,” with only a narrow exception allowing 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1:80. This blocks PC4→PC17 requests (inbound on eth2) and also blocks return traffic for PC17→PC4 (echo-replies inbound on eth2). Root cause: misconfigured iptables rules on router6 that drop all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2. Corrective fix: on router6, remove/flush those FORWARD rules or replace with proper policy (e.g., accept forwarding between eth2 and LANs with “-m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED” and required ACLs). After adjusting, ensure persistence so the firewall doesn’t revert. === END ===